hnology advances and the cost  to produce continues to drop  even countries with smaller military  budgets have been able to develop in  this area  it has been estimated that 80 000  surveillance drones and almost 2 000  attack drones will be purchased around  the world  in the next 10 years however all of  these military drones are controlled by  human operators  who decide when and who the drone will  fire upon  so what is the likelihood of humans  being removed from the loop where  ai-powered drones  will select and lock onto targets with  no human oversight  lethal autonomous weapons don't exist  yet but the technology to replace the  human with an algorithm that makes the  decision of when to shoot  does experts in machine learning and  military technology  say it would be technologically  straightforward to build robots to make  decisions  about whom to target and kill without a  human in the loop and as facial  recognition  and decision-making algorithms become  more powerful it will only get easier  according to lead ai researchers  autonomous weapons  are technically easier than self-driving  cars  they go on to say that the technology  and design required would be relatively  easy to put together in less than a  couple of years the algorithm could have  a fixed list of people  it can target and fire only if it's  highly confident that it's identified  one of these targets  or it could be trained from combat  footage to predict whether a human would  tell it to fire  or it could be taught to fire on anyone  in a war zone holding something visually  identifiable as a gun  and not wearing the uniform of friendly  forces  these technologies are being prototyped  as we speak  in the past few years the state of ai  has taken huge steps forward  facial recognition has gotten vastly  more accurate as is object recognition  two skills that are essential for lethal  autonomous weapons  new techniques have enabled ai systems  to do things that would have been  impossible just a few years ago  in fact the us army is already  developing a swarm of autonomous ai  drones  to protect combat helicopters the swarm  is modeled after  social swarming insects like bees and  ants who protect their queen  a drone nest protects the queen  helicopter at all costs  the protective swarms tasks will range  from sophisticated electronic warfare  to acting as decoys for incoming  missiles and they'll carry out these  tasks  autonomously so if the technology  already exists  why isn't there weaponized autonomous  drones being used  over time nations have come together to  prohibit particularly gruesome or  terrifying weapons  by the mid 20th century international  conventions had banned biological  and chemical weapons along with the use  of blinding laser technology  in more recent years the united nations  decided to formally address the issue of  killer robots  elon musk has been particularly vocal on  the subject warning that artificial  intelligence  is our biggest existential threat and  potentially more dangerous than nukes  stephen hawking said in 2014 that the  development of full artificial  intelligence could spell the end of the  human race  at the international convention on  conventional weapons in geneva  the 123 participating nations voted to  form a group in 2017 of governmental  experts  to look at the lethal autonomous robots  that can select targets without human  control  which could lead to a ban the fear is  that this technology would make it  easier for countries to embark on wars  and undeclared shadow wars putting  non-combatants at greater risk  basically the risk would be transferred  from the combatants to the civilians  a key ethical principle of warfare has  been one of discrimination  requiring attackers to distinguish  between combatants and civilians  but guerrilla or insurgent warfare has  become increasingly common in the recent  decades  and combatants in such situations really  wear uniforms  making it harder 